(345,'D','D','MARK_SCHEME'),
(346,'C','C','MARK_SCHEME'),
(347,NULL,'Use of $P = VI$ **or** use of $\\Delta E_{\\text{grav}} = mg\\Delta h$ (1)\n\nUse of efficiency $= \\dfrac{\\text{useful power output}}{\\text{total power input}}$ (1)\n\nEfficiency $= 0.75$ to $0.78$ (or 75% to 78%) (1)\n\nAccept use of efficiency = useful energy output / total energy input with corresponding times.\n\nExample: $P_{\\text{motor}} = 85\\times10^{-3}\\,\\text{A} \\times 3.0\\,\\text{V} = 0.255\\,\\text{W}$; $P_{\\text{block}} = 0.05\\,\\text{kg}\\times 9.81\\,\\text{N kg}^{-1}\\times 0.40\\,\\text{m s}^{-1} = 0.196\\,\\text{W}$; efficiency $= 0.196/0.255 = 0.77$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(348,NULL,'Four forces correctly labelled (1 force correctly labelled scores 1 mark; 2 or 3 forces scores 2 marks; 4 forces scores 3 marks) (3)\n\n- Up: (normal) contact force **or** reaction (force) **or** $N$ **or** $R$ **or** force of ground on child\n- Down: weight / $W$ / $mg$ **or** gravitational force\n- Left: $F$ / friction (between ground and child)\n- Right: force/pull of rope on child **or** tension / $T$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(349,NULL,'Levels-based (6 marks): up to 4 marks for indicative content (6 points = 4, 5–4 points = 3, 3–2 points = 2, 1 point = 1) plus up to 2 marks for structure and sustained lines of reasoning.\n\nIndicative content:\n- The idea that the rope is under tension\n- Team A exerts a force on the rope **and** due to N3 the rope exerts a force on Team A\n- Force of rope on Team A > frictional force for Team A\n- Team A now has a resultant force (to the right)\n- Team A accelerates (to the right) due to N1/N2\n- (This is because) the frictional force between Team B and the ground is larger **or** Team B applies a greater force (on the rope) than Team A\n\nAccept tension for \'force of rope on team\'. MP4: accept \'unbalanced\' for \'resultant\'. Accept converse for MP6, but reference to both Team A and Team B is required.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(350,NULL,'Use of $W = mg$ (1)\n\n$W = 868\\,\\text{N}$ (1)\n\nExample: mass $= 85.0\\,\\text{kg} + 3.50\\,\\text{kg} = 88.5\\,\\text{kg}$; $W = 88.5\\,\\text{kg}\\times 9.81\\,\\text{N kg}^{-1} = 868.2\\,\\text{N}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(351,NULL,'See $868\\,\\text{N}\\times 0.45\\,\\text{m}\\times\\cos 15^\\circ$ ($= 377.3\\,\\text{N m}$) (1)\n\nSee $27\\,\\text{N}\\times 2.0\\,\\text{m}\\times\\cos 75^\\circ$ ($= 13.98\\,\\text{N m}$) (1)\n\nSee $F_{\\text{w}}\\times 2.4\\,\\text{m}\\times\\cos 15^\\circ$ ($= 2.31F_{\\text{w}}$) (1)\n\nUse of principle of moments, e.g. moment of weight of base = moment of weight of post arrangement + moment of wind (1)\n\n$F_{\\text{w}} = 157$ or $158\\,\\text{N}$ (1)\n\nAccept $\\sin 75^\\circ$ for $\\cos 15^\\circ$ and $\\sin 15^\\circ$ for $\\cos 75^\\circ$; ecf for $W$ from (a).\n\nExample: $377.29 = 13.98 + 2.31F_{\\text{w}}$ so $F_{\\text{w}} = 157\\,\\text{N}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(352,NULL,'$F_{\\text{w}}$ would increase (1)\n\nThe weight of the base would be heavier/increase (1)\n\nThis increases the clockwise moment **or** this increases the moment of the (weight of the) base (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(353,NULL,'The idea that the electrons gain energy from the light (1)\n\n(As the lamp is moved towards the LDR) the intensity/brightness/illumination of light falling on the LDR increases (1)\n\nThis increases the number of (conduction) electrons (1)\n\nWhich reduces the resistance of the LDR (1)\n\nDecreasing the p.d. across the LDR **or** increasing the p.d. across the fixed resistor/voltmeter (1)\n\nMP3: accept charge carrier (density) for electrons.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(354,NULL,'p.d. across the LDR $= 3.6\\,\\text{V}$ **or** $\\dfrac{R}{R+75\\,\\Omega}$ seen **or** $\\dfrac{75}{R+75}$ seen (1)\n\nUse of $V = IR$ **or** resistance ratio $\\times 6.0\\,\\text{V}$ = corresponding p.d. (1)\n\n$R = 110\\,\\Omega$ (1)\n\nExample: $I = 2.4\\,\\text{V}/75\\,\\Omega = 0.032\\,\\text{A}$; $R = 3.6\\,\\text{V}/0.032\\,\\text{A} = 112.5\\,\\Omega$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(355,NULL,'Combined resistance of (light) bulb and LDR is about $3\\,\\Omega$ (in the dark) **or** the combined resistance is less than the resistance of bulb/LDR (1)\n\nThe combined resistance is always much less than the ($75\\,\\Omega$) fixed resistance (1)\n\nThe p.d. across the bulb will be much less than $3\\,\\text{V}$ and so the bulb will not come on (in the dark) (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(356,NULL,'Either (energy route):\n\nUse of $E_{\\text{k}} = \\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$ **and** use of $W = Fd$ (1)\n\nUse of $W = E_{\\text{k}}$ (1)\n\nDistance $= 194\\,\\text{m}$ (1)\n\nOr (suvat route):\n\nUse of $v^2 = u^2 + 2as$ with $v = 0$ to calculate the deceleration (1)\n\nUse of $v^2 = u^2 + 2as$ with $u = 25\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$ and calculated $a$ (1)\n\nDistance $= 194$ or $195\\,\\text{m}$ (1)\n\nExample: $a = -1.61\\,\\text{m s}^{-2}$; $0 = 25^2 + 2(-1.61)s$, $s = 194\\,\\text{m}$. Reverse show that max 2.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(357,NULL,'Use of $\\text{m s}^{-1} = \\dfrac{1000}{3600}\\,\\text{km h}^{-1}$ (1)\n\nUse of correct equation(s) of motion to obtain the displacement (1)\n\n$s = 55\\,\\text{m}$ (1)\n\nExample: $\\left(\\dfrac{130\\times1000}{3600}\\right)^2 = 30^2 + 2\\times3.7\\times s$, $s = 54.6\\,\\text{m}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(358,NULL,'As the speed increases, drag increases (1)\n\nThere is greater fuel consumption to maintain a higher constant speed **or** the fuel economy reduces at higher speeds to maintain a constant speed (1)\n\nStatement linking fuel economy to engine efficiency (1)\n\nMP1: accept \'air resistance\' for \'drag\'. MP3 e.g. the efficiency of the engine may increase (with speed) but the fuel economy decreases.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(359,NULL,'Use of $3600\\times\\text{W h}$ to give energy stored $= 24\\,900\\,\\text{J}$ (1)\n\nExample: $6.91\\times3600 = 24\\,876\\,\\text{J}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(360,NULL,'Use of $V = W/Q$ (1)\n\n$Q = 6510\\,\\text{C}$ (1)\n\nExample: $Q = 24\\,876\\,\\text{J}/3.82\\,\\text{V} = 6512\\,\\text{C}$ (ecf from (a)(i); show that value gives $6545\\,\\text{C}$)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(361,NULL,'Use of $Q = It$ **or** $W = VIt$ (1)\n\nUse of $\\dfrac{\\text{time in seconds}}{3600}$ (1)\n\n$t = 2.0\\,\\text{h}$ (1)\n\nExample: $t = 6512\\,\\text{C}/0.9\\,\\text{A} = 7235.6\\,\\text{s} = 2.01\\,\\text{h}$ (ecf; show that value gives $2.02\\,\\text{h}$)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(362,NULL,'The replacement charger will still have to supply the same charge ($6510\\,\\text{C}$) (1)\n\nThe replacement charging plug takes more time to charge **or** the old charging plug takes less time to charge (1)\n\nReplacement charging plug uses a lower current therefore reduces heating effect (1)\n\nThe phone may try and draw a current of $1\\,\\text{A}$ which may damage the charging plug (1)\n\nMP2: calculation using $t = Q/I$ may support this.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(363,NULL,'Comparison to $y = mx + c$ (1)\n\nIdentify that $\\eta$, $\\rho_{\\text{s}}$, $\\rho_{\\text{f}}$ and $g$ are constants (1)\n\n$c = 0$ so the graph passes through the origin **or** when $d^2 = 0$, $v = 0$ so would pass through the origin (1)\n\nMP1 e.g. $v = \\left(\\dfrac{g(\\rho_{\\text{b}}-\\rho_{\\text{f}})}{18\\eta}\\right)\\times d^2\\ (+0)$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(364,NULL,'Axes labelled with quantities and units (1)\n\nSuitable scale (1)\n\nCorrect plotting (1)\n\nLine of best fit (judged by eye) (1)\n\nMP1: $v\\,/\\,10^{-3}\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$ on $y$-axis and $d^2\\,/\\,10^{-6}\\,\\text{m}^2$ on $x$-axis','MARK_SCHEME'),
(365,NULL,'Attempt to find gradient, at least half drawn line used (1)\n\nUse of $\\eta = \\dfrac{g(\\rho_{\\text{s}}-\\rho_{\\text{f}})}{18}\\times\\dfrac{1}{\\text{gradient}}$ (1)\n\n$\\eta = 1.4$ to $1.5\\,\\text{Pa s}$ (1)\n\nCorn syrup identified as the fluid (1)\n\nMP4 to be consistent with calculated value for $\\eta$.\n\nExample: $\\eta = \\dfrac{9.81\\times(8000-1260)}{18\\times2.52\\times10^{3}} = 1.46\\,\\text{Pa s}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(366,NULL,'Graphene is only 1 atom thick so the CSA/thickness is far smaller than for a sample of steel **or** most applications need a thickness greater than one atom **or** if more than one layer is used it will be weaker / bonds between layers not strong **or** graphene is difficult to manufacture because it has only one atomic layer (1)\n\nAlthough graphene has a greater breaking stress it will break at a lower force (1)\n\n(MP1: treat references to cost/energy as neutral)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(367,NULL,'Use of depth of graphite $= 100\\times$ diameter of 1 carbon atom (1)\n\nUse of cross-sectional area $=$ depth $\\times (0.5\\times10^{-3}\\,\\text{m})$ (1)\n\nUse of $\\rho = \\dfrac{RA}{l}$ (1)\n\n$\\rho = 3.6\\times10^{-5}\\,\\Omega\\,\\text{m}$ **or** $36\\,\\mu\\Omega\\,\\text{m}$ (1)\n\nExample: depth $= 1.4\\times10^{-8}\\,\\text{m}$; $A = 7.0\\times10^{-12}\\,\\text{m}^2$; $\\rho = \\dfrac{1.029\\times10^{6}\\times7.0\\times10^{-12}}{0.200} = 3.6\\times10^{-5}\\,\\Omega\\,\\text{m}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(368,NULL,'Max 3 from:\n\nSilicon will only release a (photo)electron for a limited range of frequencies/wavelengths (1)\n\nSilicon releases only one (photo)electron per incident photon (1)\n\nGreater current (for the same illumination) in graphene (1)\n\nGraphene (cells are) more efficient **or** graphene cells could be smaller / cheaper / thinner (1)\n\nMP1: accept single frequency for limited range.','MARK_SCHEME'),
