(376,'A','A','MARK_SCHEME'),
(377,NULL,'Due to the internal resistance of the cell (1)\n\nThere is a potential difference across the internal resistance of the cell **Or** there will be \'lost volts\' **Or** $V = \\varepsilon - Ir$ (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(378,NULL,'Identifies $\\varepsilon = 1.36\\,\\text{V}$ (1)\n\nUse of $I = V/R$ with $R = 5.92\\,\\Omega$ and $V = 0.84\\,\\text{V}$ (1)\n\nUse of $\\varepsilon = V + Ir$ **Or** equivalent to determine $r$ (1)\n\n$r = 3.7\\,\\Omega$ (1)\n\nBoth are correct (because $r$ has increased and $\\varepsilon$ has decreased) (1)\n\nExample: $I = 0.84\\,\\text{V}/5.92\\,\\Omega = 0.142\\,\\text{A}$; $1.36\\,\\text{V} = 0.84\\,\\text{V} + 0.142\\,\\text{A}\\times r$; $r = 3.66\\,\\Omega$\n\nMP1 may be implied in a calculation; MP5 requires descriptions of both $r$ and $\\varepsilon$ and is conditional on MP4.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(379,NULL,'Caliper 1 gives reading to the nearest $0.01\\,\\text{mm}$ (1)\n\nCaliper 2 gives reading to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{mm}$ (1)\n\nTechnique comparison, e.g. caliper 2 involves judgement of which lines are aligned / caliper 1 gives a direct reading / caliper 2 has parallax error (1)\n\nStatement that due to variation in diameter extra resolution of caliper 1 is irrelevant **Or** percentage error/uncertainty for caliper 1 is lower (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(380,NULL,'Use of $\\pi r^2$ or $\\pi d^2/4$ (1)\n\nUse of $I = nAvq$ (1)\n\n$v = 5.8$ to $6.0\\times 10^{-7}\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$ (1)\n\nExample: $A = \\pi(12.2\\times10^{-3}\\,\\text{m})^2/4 = 1.2\\times10^{-4}\\,\\text{m}^2$; $1.9\\,\\text{A} = 1.7\\times10^{29}\\,\\text{m}^{-3}\\times1.2\\times10^{-4}\\,\\text{m}^2\\times v\\times1.6\\times10^{-19}\\,\\text{C}$; $v = 5.8\\times10^{-7}\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(381,NULL,'Use of $v = u + at$ (1)\n\nMax acceleration from 0–60 time $= 2.8\\,\\text{m s}^{-2}$ (1)\n\nExample: $\\dfrac{(60\\times1600)\\,\\text{m}}{(60\\times60)\\,\\text{s}} = 0 + a\\times 9.5\\,\\text{s}$; $a = 2.8\\,\\text{m s}^{-2}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(382,NULL,'Use of $v^2 = u^2 + 2as$ (1)\n\nMax speed with manufacturer\'s acceleration $= 18\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$ **Or** acceleration shown by police $= 3.3\\,\\text{m s}^{-2}$ (1)\n\nDecision **and** evidence required consistent with calculated values, e.g. $18.3\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$ is lower than $20\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$ so should be challenged (1)\n\nExample: $v^2 = 0^2 + 2\\times2.8\\,\\text{m s}^{-2}\\times60\\,\\text{m}$; $v = 18.3\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$ (with show-that value $3\\,\\text{m s}^{-2}$: $19.0\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(383,NULL,'Air resistance increases with speed so acceleration decreases (at higher speeds) (1)\n\nThe car could brake with greater negative acceleration/force than the positive acceleration/force (1)\n\nAccept friction for air resistance; ignore references to the mass of the car.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(384,NULL,'Use of efficiency = output energy / input energy (1)\n\nWith energy output = load $\\times$ change in height (1)\n\nEnergy input $= IVt$ (1)\n\nMay be answered in terms of power. Example: $\\Delta E_{\\text{grav}} = 4.00\\,\\text{N}\\times0.825\\,\\text{m} = 3.30\\,\\text{J}$; $W = 2.1\\,\\text{A}\\times4.3\\,\\text{V}\\times2.19\\,\\text{s} = 19.8\\,\\text{J}$; efficiency $= 3.30/19.8 = 0.167$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(385,NULL,'Max 5 from:\n\n- The time is too short to be measured by a stop watch **Or** there is reaction time (1)\n- Another variable could have been controlled (must specify e.g. current or p.d.) (1)\n- Repeats should have been taken and a mean calculated (1)\n- Identifies that there is an anomaly (at about 5 N) that has not been checked/repeated (1)\n- Smaller weight intervals (around 6 N) to identify the maximum **Or** plot/take more data points around 6 N (1)\n- Don\'t know where the maximum is so can\'t make a judgement that there is a maximum efficiency at 6 N (1)\n- The conclusion ignores the decline after 6 N (so is incomplete) (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(386,NULL,'Use of moment = force $\\times$ perpendicular distance (1)\n\nUse of clockwise moments = anticlockwise moments (1)\n\nPosition of centre of gravity $= 27\\,\\text{cm}$ from base (1)\n\nExample: $(18.5\\,\\text{kg}\\times9.81\\,\\text{N kg}^{-1})\\times x\\cos\\theta = 50\\,\\text{N}\\times0.97\\,\\text{m}\\times\\cos\\theta$; $x = 0.27\\,\\text{m}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(387,NULL,'Use of $\\cos\\theta$ = vertical force $\\div$ applied force (1)\n\nAnswer $55\\,\\text{N}$ (1)\n\nExample: $\\cos 25^\\circ = 50\\,\\text{N}\\div F$; $F = 55.2\\,\\text{N}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(388,NULL,'Correct use of trigonometrical function to determine force in direction of motion (1)\n\nUse of $W = Fs$ **and** $P = W/t$ **Or** use of $v = s/t$ **and** $P = Fv$ (1)\n\n$P = 83\\,\\text{W}$ (1)\n\nExample: $F = 55.2\\,\\text{N}\\times\\sin25^\\circ = 23.3\\,\\text{N}$; $P = \\dfrac{23.3\\,\\text{N}\\times15\\,\\text{m}}{4.2\\,\\text{s}} = 83.2\\,\\text{W}$ (ecf from (b)(i))','MARK_SCHEME'),
(389,NULL,'Use of $R = V/I$ using pair of points from the graph (1)\n\n$R = 4.9\\,\\Omega$ (1)\n\nExample: $R = 7.00\\,\\text{V}/1.44\\,\\text{A} = 4.86\\,\\Omega$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(390,NULL,'Calculated resistance point plotted correctly at $(4.9\\,\\Omega, 7.0\\,\\text{V})$ (1)\n\nResistance increases (constantly) with potential difference (1)\n\nPositive intercept with $y$-axis (less than value from (a)) (1)\n\nMP3 conditional on MP2. (MS sketch: straight line rising from a positive intercept to $4.9\\,\\Omega$ at $7.0\\,\\text{V}$.)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(391,NULL,'Levels-based: 4 marks for indicative content (6 points → 4, 5–4 → 3, 3–2 → 2, 1 → 1) + up to 2 marks for structure and lines of reasoning.\n\nIndicative content:\n\n- (As $V$ increases) acceleration/velocity/energy of electrons increases\n- greater energy transfer in collisions with lattice/ions\n- increasing the temperature of metal/filament/ions\n- amplitude of vibrations of lattice/ions increases\n- collision (rate) between lattice/ions and electrons increases **Or** shorter distance between collisions **Or** greater chance of collision\n- due to the increase in resistance, current doesn\'t increase in proportion to potential difference **Or** the ratio $I/V$ decreases\n\nAccept charge carriers for electrons. MP1: don\'t award for \'increased rate of flow\'. MP2: must have idea of increased energy transfer between electrons and ions.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(392,NULL,'Use of $\\lambda = h/p$ **and** $v = f\\lambda$ (1)\n\nMomentum of photon $= 3.3\\times10^{-27}\\,\\text{N s}$ (1)\n\nExample: $p = hf/c = 6.63\\times10^{-34}\\,\\text{J s}\\times1.5\\times10^{15}\\,\\text{Hz}\\div3.00\\times10^{8}\\,\\text{m s}^{-1} = 3.315\\times10^{-27}\\,\\text{N s}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(393,NULL,'Momentum transfer $= 6.6\\times10^{-27}\\,\\text{N s}$ (1) [ecf from (i)]','MARK_SCHEME'),
(394,NULL,'Use of $hf = \\varphi + \\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2_{\\max}$ (1)\n\nUse of $E_k = \\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$ (1)\n\n$v = 8.4\\times10^{5}\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$ (1)\n\nExample: $hf = 6.63\\times10^{-34}\\,\\text{J s}\\times1.5\\times10^{15}\\,\\text{Hz} = 9.95\\times10^{-19}\\,\\text{J}$; $hf-\\varphi = 3.25\\times10^{-19}\\,\\text{J} = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\times9.11\\times10^{-31}\\,\\text{kg}\\times v^2$; $v = 8.4\\times10^{5}\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(395,NULL,'Use of $p = mv$ (1)\n\nMomentum of photoelectron $= 7.7\\times10^{-25}\\,\\text{N s}$ (1)\n\nExample: $p = 9.11\\times10^{-31}\\,\\text{kg}\\times8.4\\times10^{5}\\,\\text{m s}^{-1} = 7.68\\times10^{-25}\\,\\text{N s}$ (using show-that value: $7.3\\times10^{-25}\\,\\text{N s}$)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(396,NULL,'The change in momentum of the graphene oxide is the same as the change in momentum of the photoelectron (1)\n\nSo the (change in) momentum is much larger for the photoelectron than for the reflected photon (1)\n\nAccept converse statement and answer consistent with candidate\'s values in (a) and (b).','MARK_SCHEME'),
(397,NULL,'Processing of data to calculate change in length (1)\n\nAxes with labels & units (accept force for weight) (1)\n\nScales (1)\n\nPlots (1)\n\nLine of best fit (1)\n\n| Weight / N | Compression / cm |\n|---|---|\n| 0.00 | 0.0 |\n| 2.00 | 0.3 |\n| 4.00 | 0.7 |\n| 6.00 | 1.1 |\n| 8.00 | 1.4 |\n| 10.00 | 1.8 |\n\nMP2: only for a graph of weight against compression (m or cm). MP3: scales only in 1, 2, 4, 5 and covering at least half the paper. MP4: 2 mm square tolerance, check all points.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(398,NULL,'States that best fit line is through the origin (1)\n\nSo it fits Hooke\'s law because extension is proportional to force (1)\n\nUses corresponding values from best fit line from (a)(i) to determine gradient (1)\n\nSpring constant $= 10.0\\,\\text{N}/0.0176\\,\\text{m} = 568\\,\\text{N m}^{-1}$ (which, to 1 s.f., is the stated answer) (1)\n\nIf plunger position plotted in (a)(i) only MP2 may be awarded. MP3: values from at least half way along line. MP4 conditional on MP3; allow any value rounding to 600 (1 s.f.).','MARK_SCHEME'),
(399,NULL,'Use of $\\Delta F = k\\Delta x$ (1)\n\nUse of $\\Delta E_{\\text{el}} = \\tfrac{1}{2}F\\Delta x$ (1)\n\nUse of $E_k = \\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$ (1)\n\n$v = 6.7$ to $6.8\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$ (1)\n\nExample: $\\Delta F = 610\\,\\text{N m}^{-1}\\times0.054\\,\\text{m} = 32.94\\,\\text{N}$; $\\Delta E_{\\text{el}} = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\times32.94\\,\\text{N}\\times0.054\\,\\text{m} = 0.90\\,\\text{J}$; $0.90\\,\\text{J} = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\times(0.0041+0.0354)\\,\\text{kg}\\times v^2$; $v = 6.75\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(400,NULL,'Work may be done against friction (by the spring/marble) **Or** KE is gained by the spring **Or** GPE gained by the piston **and** marble **Or** the light gate must be above the launch position so the marble is already accelerating downwards **Or** statement of friction between two specified parts in launch system (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(401,'D','D','MARK_SCHEME'),
(402,'B','B','MARK_SCHEME'),
(403,'A','A','MARK_SCHEME'),
(404,'B','B','MARK_SCHEME'),
(405,'C','C','MARK_SCHEME'),
(406,'C','C','MARK_SCHEME'),
(407,'A','A','MARK_SCHEME'),
(408,'C','C','MARK_SCHEME'),
(409,NULL,'A statement applying the conservation of energy to the circuit (1)\n\nUse of Ohm\'s law for each term / individual p.d. leading to the cancelling of currents in the equation (1)\n\n$R_T = R_1 + R_2 + R_3$ (1)\n\nExample of calculation: $\\varepsilon = V_1 + V_2 + V_3$; $\\varepsilon = IR_T = IR_1 + IR_2 + IR_3$; $R_T = R_1 + R_2 + R_3$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(410,NULL,'**Either**\n\nUse of equation(s) to determine the total resistance of the voltmeter and $40\\,\\Omega$ resistor in parallel ($34.3\\,\\Omega$), i.e. potential divider formula or Ohm\'s law (1)\n\nUse of $\\dfrac{1}{R_T} = \\dfrac{1}{R_1} + \\dfrac{1}{R_2}$ (1)\n\n$R_V = 240\\,(\\Omega)$ (1)\n\n**Or**\n\nUse of Ohm\'s law to determine the current through voltmeter ($0.0075\\,\\text{A}$), i.e. current in $40\\,\\Omega$ resistor calculated ($0.045\\,\\text{A}$) and subtracted from current in $80\\,\\Omega$ resistor ($0.0525\\,\\text{A}$) (1)\n\nUse of Ohm\'s law with $1.8\\,\\text{V}$ to calculate the resistance of the voltmeter (1)\n\n$R_V = 240\\,(\\Omega)$ (1)\n\nExample of calculation: $\\left(\\dfrac{R}{80\\,\\Omega + R}\\right) 6\\,\\text{V} = 1.8\\,\\text{V}$, $R = 34.29\\,\\Omega$; $\\dfrac{1}{34.29\\,\\Omega} = \\dfrac{1}{40\\,\\Omega} + \\dfrac{1}{R_V}$; $R_V = 240.2\\,\\Omega$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(411,NULL,'Weight of the picture is equal to the vertical component of tension (1)\n\n$mg/2 = T\\cos\\theta$, where $\\theta$ is the angle between the wire and the vertical (1)\n\nAs the angle (to the vertical) is smaller in arrangement 1, the cosine of the angle will be larger (1)\n\nArrangement 1 as the tension in the wire is lower than in arrangement 2 (1)\n\nMP1 may be implied in an equation, accept $mg = T\\cos\\theta$. Answer can be in terms of $\\sin\\theta$ if $\\theta$ defined as the angle between the wire and the horizontal, e.g. $mg = 2T\\sin\\theta$. MP4 conditional on MP2 or MP3.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(412,NULL,'The weight does not act through the nail/pivot, or the centre of gravity is not in line with/below the nail/pivot, or there is a perpendicular distance between the weight and the nail/pivot (1)\n\nThere is now a moment of the weight, or the anticlockwise moment is greater than the clockwise moment (1)\n\nThe idea that the picture stops moving when the c of g is below the nail (1)\n\nAccept centre of mass for centre of gravity. MP3 accept: the turning moment being 0, or the clockwise moments equal to the anticlockwise moments.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(413,NULL,'Use of $v = s/t$ for the horizontal motion, or see $u_h = 25\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$ (1)\n\nUse of $s = ut + \\tfrac{1}{2}at^2$ with $s = 0$, or $v = u + at$ with $t = 1.0\\,\\text{s}$, or see $u_v = 9.81\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$ (1)\n\nCombining of horizontal velocity and vertical velocity expressions, or see $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{9.81}{25}$ (1)\n\n$\\theta = 21^\\circ$ (1)\n\nExample of calculation: $u_h = \\dfrac{50\\,\\text{m}}{2.0\\,\\text{s}} = 25\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$; $0 = u_v(2.0\\,\\text{s}) + \\tfrac{1}{2}(-9.81\\,\\text{m s}^{-2})(2.0\\,\\text{s})^2$, $u_v = 9.81\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$; $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{9.81}{25}$, $\\theta = 21.4^\\circ$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(414,NULL,'Construction of vector diagram with $2\\,\\text{N}$/weight and $9\\,\\text{N}$/catapult force labelled and all three directions shown (1)\n\nCorrect scaling of $9\\,\\text{N}$ and $2\\,\\text{N}$ forces (1)\n\nMagnitude $= 7.6\\,\\text{N}$ to $8.0\\,\\text{N}$ (1)\n\nDirection $= 27^\\circ$ to $31^\\circ$ (1)\n\nMP2: award if MP3 awarded; otherwise the ratio of the lengths should lie between 4.3 and 4.8. If no diagram, only MP3/4 can be awarded if answers obtained by calculation.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(415,NULL,'Use of $p = mv$ (1)\n\nUse of principle of conservation of momentum (1)\n\nMagnitude of velocity $= 0.2\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$ with direction to the left (1)\n\nMP1: see $0.3m$, $0.7m$ or $2mv$. MP3: accept \'in the initial direction of glider 2\' for \'to the left\'.\n\nExample of calculation (taking the initial direction of glider 1 as positive): $0.3m - 0.7m = 2mv$, $v = -0.2\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(416,NULL,'Glider 1 exerts this force on glider 2, so according to N3 (1)\n\nGlider 2 will exert an (equal and) opposite force on glider 1 (1)\n\nThere is now a resultant force on glider 1 (1)\n\nGlider 1 accelerates according to N1, or glider 1 now moves to the left according to N1 (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(417,NULL,'The (voltmeter) reading will increase (1)\n\nResistance increases (with length), or resistance $\\propto$ length (1)\n\nMP2: accept idea of a potential divider, i.e. the ratio of BC to the total length AD will be greater, so the proportion of the total voltage will be greater ($\\frac{BC}{AD}V$). Do not award if there is also a reference to resistivity increasing.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(418,NULL,'Use of $V = IR$ (1)\n\nUse of $R = \\rho l/A$ (1)\n\n(Min) resistivity $= 160\\,(\\Omega\\,\\text{m})$, or (max) resistivity $= 730\\,(\\Omega\\,\\text{m})$ (1)\n\nCompacted clay pathways and limestone are present in the soil (1)\n\nExample of calculation: $R_{\\text{min}} = \\dfrac{1.8\\,\\text{V}}{9.5 \\times 10^{-3}\\,\\text{A}} = 189.5\\,\\Omega$; $R_{\\text{max}} = \\dfrac{8.0\\,\\text{V}}{9.5 \\times 10^{-3}\\,\\text{A}} = 842.1\\,\\Omega$; $\\rho_{\\text{min}} = \\dfrac{RA}{l} = \\dfrac{189.5 \\times 0.650\\,\\text{m}^2}{0.75\\,\\text{m}} = 164.2\\,\\Omega\\,\\text{m}$; $\\rho_{\\text{max}} = \\dfrac{842.1 \\times 0.650\\,\\text{m}^2}{0.75\\,\\text{m}} = 729.8\\,\\Omega\\,\\text{m}$. Conclusion to be consistent with calculated values.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(419,NULL,'Attempt to find area under the graph (1)\n\nLength from $18\\,000\\,\\text{m}$ to $20\\,000\\,\\text{m}$ (1)\n\nComparison of calculated value to $23\\,\\text{km}$, e.g. the length is long enough (1)\n\nMP1: use of triangles or counting squares. MP3: conclusion to be consistent with calculated value.\n\nExample of calculation: area under the graph (counting large squares) $= 18.7 \\times 100\\,\\text{m s}^{-1} \\times 10\\,\\text{s} = 18\\,700\\,\\text{m}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(420,NULL,'$26$–$28\\,\\text{s}$ (1)\n\nUnit required.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(421,NULL,'Use of gradient of the graph between 28 and 46 s (1)\n\nAcceleration $= 16$–$17\\,\\text{m s}^{-2}$ (1)\n\nExample of calculation: gradient of tangent $= \\dfrac{490\\,\\text{m s}^{-1} - 0\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}}{52\\,\\text{s} - 22\\,\\text{s}}$; acceleration $= 16.3\\,\\text{m s}^{-2}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(422,NULL,'Use of $\\Sigma F = ma$ using $a$ from (ii) (1)\n\n$\\Sigma F = (89 + 120) \\times 10^{3}\\,\\text{N} - \\text{frictional force}$ (1)\n\nFrictional force $= 80\\,\\text{kN}$ to $84\\,\\text{kN}$ (full ecf for acceleration) (1)\n\nExample of calculation: $(89 + 120) \\times 10^{3}\\,\\text{N} - F = 7790\\,\\text{kg} \\times 16.3\\,\\text{m s}^{-2}$, $F = 82.0 \\times 10^{3}\\,\\text{N}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(423,NULL,'Levels-based mark scheme: indicative content (6 points = 4 marks, 5–4 = 3, 3–2 = 2, 1 = 1, 0 = 0) plus up to 2 marks for structure and sustained lines of reasoning (2 = coherent, logical structure with fully sustained reasoning; 1 = partially structured with some linkages; 0 = no linkages, unstructured).\n\nIndicative content:\n\n- At greater speed, the drag force is greater\n- Resultant force decreases, or acceleration decreases\n- When the rocket is started the (resultant) force/thrust increases\n- The mass/weight of the car decreases as fuel is used up\n- Increasing the acceleration (for a given applied force)\n- When the brakes are applied, there is a deceleration, or when the brakes are applied the resultant force is in the opposite direction, or deceleration (due to drag forces) decreases due to smaller drag forces acting on the car at lower speeds','MARK_SCHEME'),
(424,NULL,'Any one of (1):\n\n- No repeats\n- Or range too small\n- Or readings should be repeated (and mean calculated)\n- Or separations less than $0.50\\,\\text{m}$ should be used\n- Or separations greater than $1.00\\,\\text{m}$ should be used\n- Or distances should be recorded to the nearest mm\n- Or smaller intervals (for the separation) should be used\n\nDo not credit: more readings should be taken; references to the number of decimal places.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(425,NULL,'Axes labelled with quantities and units (1)\n\nSuitable scale (1)\n\nCorrect plotting (1)\n\nLine of best fit (1)\n\nGraph may be landscape and axes either way round. MP2: suitable scale – plotted points take up at least half the graph paper in both directions.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(426,NULL,'Use of gradient, or use of 1/gradient (if axes swapped) (1)\n\nSpeed of sound $= 280$–$310\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$ (1)\n\nMP1: only award for a $y/x$ calculation if the graph passes through the origin.\n\nExample of calculation: speed of sound $= \\dfrac{0.96\\,\\text{m} - 0.04\\,\\text{m}}{(3.0 - 0.0) \\times 10^{-3}\\,\\text{s}} = 307\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(427,NULL,'Value obtained is lower than accepted value (1)\n\nMax 2 from:\n\n- Measured times are too small (1)\n- Large percentage uncertainty for time (1)\n- Parallax when reading from the metre rule (1)\n- The distance not measured to same position on each microphone (1)\n\nMP1: accept answer consistent with calculated value but do not accept \'different\' for greater/lower. Ignore references to temperature.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(428,NULL,'Oscillations/vibrations (of the light) are in one plane only (1)\n\nPlane includes the direction of energy transfer, or plane includes the direction of travel/propagation (1)\n\nAccept: oscillations/vibrations (of the light) are in one direction only; perpendicular to the direction of propagation/travel, or perpendicular to the direction of energy transfer. Allow labelled diagrams for each marking point.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(429,NULL,'The (angle of polarisation of the) filters are $90^\\circ$ to one another (1)\n\n**Either** if plane of polarisation of light is rotated (by $90^\\circ$) when it passes through the crystal (with no p.d. across it), it can still pass through the upper filter (1)\n\n**Or** if plane of polarisation of light is not rotated (by $90^\\circ$) when it passes through the crystal (with a p.d. across it), it cannot pass through the upper filter (1)\n\nMP2: it must be clear as to whether the candidate is describing a light screen or a dark screen.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(430,NULL,'Use of $I = P/A$ (1)\n\n$P = 0.014\\,\\text{W}$ (1)\n\nExample of calculation: $P = 7.8\\,\\text{W m}^{-2} \\times 1.8 \\times 10^{-3}\\,\\text{m}^2 = 0.014\\,\\text{W}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(431,NULL,'Use of $P = VI$ (1)\n\nUse of efficiency $= \\dfrac{\\text{useful power output}}{\\text{total power input}}$ (1)\n\nEfficiency $= 0.19$ or $0.20$ or $19\\%$ or $20\\%$ (1)\n\nExample of calculation: power input into LED $= 3.6\\,\\text{V} \\times 20 \\times 10^{-3}\\,\\text{A} = 0.072\\,\\text{W}$; efficiency $= \\dfrac{0.014\\,\\text{W}}{0.072\\,\\text{W}} = 0.194$. ecf from (b)(i) for the power output of LED.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(432,NULL,'X is brittle at greater stresses/forces (1)\n\nY will deform plastically at greater stresses/forces (1)\n\nThe Young modulus for X is greater than Y (1)\n\nA screen made from material Y would be more suitable as it is more flexible (1)\n\nAccept converse for MP3 and MP4. MP4: accept less stiff for flexible.','MARK_SCHEME'),
