(441,NULL,'- Diode (1)\n- It conducts/allows current in one direction only (1)\n- The diode conducts when the p.d. is beyond 0.2–0.7 V **Or** for negative p.d.s the resistance is (very) high **Or** in the reverse/backward direction the resistance is (very) high **Or** at breakdown/large voltage the diode will conduct in the reverse direction (1)\n\nMP3: allow threshold voltage','MARK_SCHEME'),
(442,NULL,'- Calculation of gradient of the graph (1)\n- Use of $s = \\tfrac{1}{2}at^2$ to obtain value for $g$ (1)  [use of $\\dfrac{2}{\\text{gradient}}$]\n- Total uncertainty $= 7\\%$ (1)  [$3\\% + 3\\% + 1\\%$]\n- Calculation of % difference **Or** range of calculated $g$ (1)\n- Judgment on accuracy of experiment with reason (1)  [e.g. comparison of total uncertainty with % difference, or comparison of calculated range with $9.81\\,\\text{m s}^{-2}$]\n\nExample of calculation:\nGradient $= \\dfrac{0.385\\,\\text{s}^2 - 0.06\\,\\text{s}^2}{1.8\\,\\text{m} - 0.4\\,\\text{m}} = 0.232\\,\\text{s}^2\\,\\text{m}^{-1}$\n\n$g = \\dfrac{2}{0.232\\,\\text{s}^2\\,\\text{m}^{-1}} = 8.67\\,\\text{m s}^{-2}$\n\nPercentage difference $= \\left(\\dfrac{9.81 - 8.67}{9.81}\\right) \\times 100 = 12\\%$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(443,NULL,'- Use of $\\dfrac{1}{R} = \\dfrac{1}{R_1} + \\dfrac{1}{R_2}$ to determine the total resistance of the parallel branch (1)\n- $R_\\text{TP} = 8.0\\,\\text{k}\\Omega$ (1)\n- Comparison of measured to actual resistance (1)  [e.g. $7.98\\,\\text{k}\\Omega$ is significantly less than $9.7\\,\\text{k}\\Omega$, so unsuitable]\n\n**OR**\n- Same p.d. across thermistor and voltmeter (1)\n- Calculation of ratio of currents (1)\n- States that current through voltmeter is significant (1)\n\nMP3 dependent on MP2.\n\nExample: $\\dfrac{1}{R_\\text{TP}} = \\dfrac{1}{9.7\\,\\text{k}\\Omega} + \\dfrac{1}{45\\,\\text{k}\\Omega}$, $R_\\text{TP} = 7.98\\,\\text{k}\\Omega$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(444,NULL,'- Current flows through the voltmeter (1)\n- But in the new arrangement, the ammeter would read only the current passing through the thermistor **Or** current through ammeter equals current through thermistor (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(445,NULL,'- As temperature increases number of (free) charge carriers (in thermistor) increases so its resistance decreases **Or** as temperature increases number of charge carriers in conduction band increases so (thermistor) resistance decreases (1)\n- Thermistor resistance as a proportion of total resistance decreases **Or** current increases so p.d. across resistor increases (1)\n- P.D. (across thermistor) decreases (1)\n\nAccept electrons for charge carriers.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(446,NULL,'- Use of efficiency $= \\dfrac{\\text{useful power output}}{\\text{total power input}}$ (1)\n- Use of $\\Delta E_\\text{grav} = mg\\Delta h$ (1)\n- Use of $E_\\text{k} = \\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$ (1)\n- Use of (output) power $= \\dfrac{\\Delta E_\\text{grav}}{1\\,\\text{s}} + \\dfrac{E_\\text{k}}{1\\,\\text{s}}$ (1)\n- $v = 7.8\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$ (1)\n\nExample: Output power $= 0.76 \\times 160\\,\\text{W} = 121.6\\,\\text{W}$; $121.6 = (3.5)(9.81)(0.45) + \\tfrac{1}{2}(3.5)v^2$; $v = 7.78\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(447,NULL,'- Use of trig to determine the vertical or horizontal component of the initial velocity (1)\n- Use of equation(s) of motion to determine time the water is in air (1)\n- Use of $s = vt$ (1)\n- $s = 4.8\\,\\text{m}$ (1)\n\nAllow ecf from (a). (Using show that value, $s = 5.0\\,\\text{m}$)\n\nExample: $u_\\text{h} = 7.8 \\sin 25 = 3.30\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$; $u_\\text{v} = 7.8 \\cos 25 = 7.07\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$; $0 = 7.07t + \\tfrac{1}{2}(-9.81)t^2$, $t = 1.44\\,\\text{s}$; $s = 1.44 \\times 3.30 = 4.75\\,\\text{m}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(448,NULL,'- There is friction between the water and the pipes (1)\n- This will reduce the (initial) velocity of the water (1)\n\nMP2 is dependent on MP1.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(449,NULL,'MAX 2\n- Statement describing $740\\cos 20$ as the (perpendicular) component of weight of the hiker **and** $W\\cos 20$ as the (perpendicular) component of the weight of the bag (1)\n- $2R$ is the push of the ground on the hiker (1)  [accept reaction force]\n- Use of $\\Sigma F = 0$ with reference to hiker being stationary (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(450,NULL,'- See $740\\,\\text{N} \\times 0.25\\,\\text{m} \\times \\cos 20$ ($= 173.8\\,\\text{N m}$) (1)\n- See $W \\times 0.10\\,\\text{m} \\times \\cos 20$ ($= 0.0940W\\,\\text{N m}$) (1)\n- See $R \\times 0.40\\,\\text{m}$ ($= 0.40R\\,\\text{N m}$) **Or** $0.5(740\\cos 20 + W\\cos 20)$ (1)\n- Use of principle of moments, e.g. moment of weight of man = moment of weight of bag + moment of $R$ (1)\n- Use of equation of the resultant force with the equation obtained in MP4 **OR** use of principle of moments about another point with the equation obtained in MP4 (1)\n- $W = 120\\,\\text{N}$ (1)\n\nExample: $173.8 = 0.40R + 0.0940W$ so $R = 435 - 0.235W$; resultant force: $R = 347.7 + 0.470W$; $0.705W = 87.3$; $W = 124\\,\\text{N}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(451,NULL,'- The position of the centre of gravity moves to the left/backwards **Or** the perpendicular distance (from O) would be greater (1)  [accept lower for to the left]\n- The moment of the bag (about O) increases so the moment of $R$ (and the size of $R$) decreases to preserve equilibrium (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(452,NULL,'Extended response (6 marks): up to 4 marks for indicative content (6 points = 4, 5–4 = 3, 3–2 = 2, 1 = 1) plus up to 2 marks for structure and sustained line of reasoning.\n\nIndicative content:\n- Sphere A applies a force to sphere B (on impact)\n- According to Newton\'s third law sphere B will apply an (equal and) opposite force to sphere A\n- This force opposes the motion of sphere A\n- Sphere A decelerates, according to N2\n- The (resultant) force on sphere B accelerates B\n- The forces/impulse acting (on the spheres) are equal so the change in speeds/momentum are the same for each sphere','MARK_SCHEME'),
(453,NULL,'- Measurement of change in height of sphere A (1)  [initial decrease in height from photo $= 2.9 \\pm 0.1\\,\\text{cm}$]\n- Actual height $= \\dfrac{\\text{image height} \\times 11}{4.8}$ (1)  [height of frame in photo $= 4.8 \\pm 0.1\\,\\text{cm}$]\n- Use of $E_\\text{k}$ gained $= E_\\text{grav}$ lost to determine $v$ (1)\n- Use of $p = mv$ (1)\n- $p_\\text{A} = 0.025\\,\\text{kg m s}^{-1}$ (1)\n\nMP2–4 award even if measurement for the height is out of range. MP3: use of equation of motion scores 0. Accept $p_\\text{A}$ in range $0.024$–$0.026\\,\\text{kg m s}^{-1}$.\n\nExample: $h_\\text{A} = \\dfrac{2.9 \\times 11}{4.8} = 6.6\\,\\text{cm}$; $\\tfrac{1}{2}(0.022)v_\\text{A}^2 = 0.022 \\times 9.81 \\times 6.6\\times 10^{-2}$; $v_\\text{A} = 1.14\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$; $p_\\text{A} = 0.022 \\times 1.14 = 0.025\\,\\text{kg m s}^{-1}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(454,NULL,'- Processing of data to calculate $1/d$ (1)\n- Axes with labels and units (1)\n- Scales (can include $(0, 0)$) (1)\n- Plots (1)\n- Line of best fit (1)\n\nProcessed values $d^{-1}$ / m$^{-1}$: Honey 29, Corn syrup 25, Seawater 20, Vegetable oil 18, Baby oil 17','MARK_SCHEME'),
(455,NULL,'- Graph ($\\rho_\\text{L}$ against $1/d$) is a straight line (1)\n- Through the origin (1)\n\nFor MP2 accept statement that line should go through the origin.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(456,NULL,'- Determine gradient from line of best fit (1)\n- Use of gradient $= \\rho_\\text{s}x$ (1)\n- $\\rho_\\text{s} = 550$–$700\\,\\text{kg m}^{-3}$ (1)\n- Type of wood identified appropriately from their $\\rho_\\text{s}$ (1)\n\nExample: Gradient $= \\dfrac{1500\\,\\text{kg m}^{-3}}{30\\,\\text{m}^{-1}} = 50\\,\\text{kg m}^{-2}$; $50 = 0.09\\rho_\\text{s}$; $\\rho_\\text{s} = 556\\,\\text{kg m}^{-3}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(457,NULL,'- (Moving) electrons collide with lattice/ions (1)\n- Transfer of energy to (lattice) ions so they vibrate with larger amplitude/speed (and the temperature increases) (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(458,NULL,'- Electrons/ions in the tube collide with mercury/phosphor atoms and excite electrons (in the mercury/phosphor atoms) (1)\n- Energy is released in the form of photons as the electrons move back down (to the ground state) (1)\n\nMention of work function scores 0. MP2: allow de-excite for move back down.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(459,NULL,'- Use of cross-sectional area $= \\pi r^2$ **Or** $\\dfrac{\\pi d^2}{4}$ (1)\n- Use of $R = \\dfrac{\\rho l}{A}$ (1)\n- Correct use of factor of 14 (1)\n- Use of $P = \\dfrac{V^2}{R}$ (1)\n- $P = 52\\,\\text{W}$ (1)\n\nExample: $A = \\pi(1.9\\times 10^{-5})^2 = 1.134\\times 10^{-9}\\,\\text{m}^2$; $R = \\dfrac{(5.6\\times 10^{-8})(1.6)}{1.134\\times 10^{-9}} = 79.01\\,\\Omega$; $R_\\text{max} = 14 \\times 79.01 = 1106.2\\,\\Omega$; $P = \\dfrac{240^2}{1106.2} = 52.1\\,\\text{W}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(460,NULL,'**Either**\n- Initially (the resistance is low so) current will be the greatest (1)\n- As $P = I^2R$, the greatest power is transferred (1)\n- Change in current has more effect as it is squared (1)\n- Heating effect greatest when $R$ is lowest, so breaks when switched on (1)\n\n**OR**\n- p.d. is constant (1)\n- $P = V^2/R$ (1)\n- Power is greatest when $R$ is lowest (1)\n- Heating effect is greatest when $R$ is lowest, so breaks when switched on (1)\n\n**OR**\n- p.d. is constant (1)\n- Initially (resistance is low so) current will be the greatest (1)\n- As $P = IV$ the greatest power is transferred (1)\n- Heating effect is greatest when $R$ is lowest, so breaks when switched on (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
