(477,NULL,'Use smaller increments for resistance (1)\n\nAround area of peak (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(478,NULL,'Connect thermistor to an ohmmeter to measure its resistance (1) [alt based on ammeter/voltmeter combination]\n\nUse a water bath, e.g. beaker of water, and immerse the thermistor (1)\n\nMeasure the temperature of the water using a thermometer (1)\n\nVary temperature and measure the resistance at fixed intervals (1) [alternative: vary $T$ using boiling water from a kettle and allow to cool]\n\nThermometer held in retort stand to make beaker stable **Or** leads clamped to make beaker stable (1) [alternative safety: plenty of space around apparatus **Or** safety glasses in case of splashes **Or** heatproof mat as boiling water]\n\nDetailed point such as stir water (1) [alternative detail: eye level with thermometer **Or** adjust ohmmeter for maximum sig figs without final figure varying randomly **Or** allow time for thermistor to reach water temperature **Or** keep thermometer and thermistor close together]','MARK_SCHEME'),
(479,NULL,'Max 2 from:\n\nResistance values have been taken every $10\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$; take more values, say at $25\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$ and $35\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$, when $R$ is changing more (1)\n\nWiden the range – can find $R$ up to $100\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$, i.e. boiling water **Or** decrease $t$ below room temperature by adding ice (1)\n\nRepeat experiment measuring $R$ at the same temperatures then average $R$\'s (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(480,NULL,'Increased temperature causes more energy transfer to lattice ions / atoms (1)\n\nMore charge carriers released / available (1)\n\n$I = nAvq$, so relative increase in $I$ (1)\n\nReference to $R = V/I$ to justify decrease in $R$ (1)\n\n[Or reverse argument]','MARK_SCHEME'),
(481,NULL,'Efficiency shouldn\'t have any units **Or** efficiency should be a ratio of energies **Or** a ratio of powers **Or** number $< 1$ (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(482,NULL,'Quote $P = E/t$ (1)\n\n(k)W is unit of $P$ and h unit of $t$ so equivalent to $E$ (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(483,NULL,'Use of efficiency = useful energy output / energy input (1)\n\nUses energy input is power (kW) $\\times$ time (h) (1)\n\nEfficiency $= 0.87$ (1)\n\nExample: efficiency $= \\dfrac{22\\,\\text{kW h}}{3.6\\,\\text{kW} \\times 7\\,\\text{h}} = 0.873$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(484,NULL,'Converts battery input to a cost (1)\n\nComparison of petrol and electric cost for same range **Or** comparison of petrol and electric range for same cost (1)\n\nExample: cost of charging $= 3.6\\,\\text{kW} \\times 7\\,\\text{h} \\times 13\\,\\text{p} = £3.28$; cost per km $= \\frac{3.28}{129} = 2.54\\,\\text{p}$ (or $0.195\\,\\text{kW h} \\times 13\\,\\text{p/kW h} = 2.54\\,\\text{p}$)\n\nPetrol car: cost per km $= \\frac{1.20}{21} = 5.71\\,\\text{p}$\n\nAlternative: cost for 129 km is £3.276 electric and £7.36 petrol','MARK_SCHEME'),
(485,NULL,'No exhaust particulates **Or** no lead emission **Or** less carbon dioxide emission **Or** less greenhouse gases **Or** no polluting gases (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(486,NULL,'Use of $E_k = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$ (1)\n\nUse of $P = E/t$ (1)\n\nPower required is $51\\,\\text{kW}$, less than engine power so it does have enough power (1)\n\nExample: $E_k = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 1500\\,\\text{kg} \\times (28\\,\\text{m s}^{-1})^2 = 5.88 \\times 10^5\\,\\text{J}$; $P = 5.88 \\times 10^5\\,\\text{J} / 11.5\\,\\text{s} = 51.1\\,\\text{kW}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(487,NULL,'Cell connected to both ends of paper with correct polarity (1)\n\nVoltmeter with one end connected to 0 V end of paper and other to halfway along narrow strip of paper (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(488,NULL,'Assesses coherent and logically structured answer with linkages and fully-sustained reasoning. Indicative content:\n\nUse of $R = \\rho l / A$\n\nThe wider section will be half the resistance of the narrow section\n\nThe p.d. will be divided into $0.5\\,\\text{V}$ across wide section and $1.0\\,\\text{V}$ across the narrow section\n\nThe potential will vary linearly along each resistance section (as $R$ is proportional to $l$)\n\nThis is because current is constant throughout length\n\nWhen the voltmeter is across the whole length it will read the p.d. across the cell / $1.5\\,\\text{V}$ **Or** when the voltmeter terminals are together it will read $0\\,\\text{V}$\n\nMarking: 6 IC points → 4 IC + 2 linkage = 6; 5 → 3+2 = 5; 4 → 3+1 = 4; 3 → 2+1 = 3; 2 → 2+0 = 2; 1 → 1+0 = 1; 0 → 0','MARK_SCHEME'),
(489,NULL,'Measure distance from grating to screen $l$ and from centre to dot $x$ (1)\n\nUse $\\tan\\theta = x/l$ to determine $\\theta$ (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(490,NULL,'Axes with labels (1)\n\nScales (1) [only in 1, 2, 4, 5 and must cover at least half of paper]\n\nPlots (1) [2 mm square tolerance, check all points]\n\nLine of best fit (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(491,NULL,'Calculation of a gradient (1)\n\nUse gradient $= d/\\lambda$ (1)\n\nUse $d = 0.001/300$ (1)\n\nWavelength $= 6.3 \\times 10^{-7}\\,\\text{m}$ (1)\n\nExample: gradient $= \\frac{4.0}{0.76} = 5.26$; $\\frac{0.001}{300} = 5.26 \\times \\lambda$; $\\lambda = 6.3 \\times 10^{-7}\\,\\text{m}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(492,NULL,'Calculate difference in positions on $y$ axis (1)\n\nUse velocity = distance / 0.1 s (1)\n\ne.g. $v = (3.0 - 2.0)\\,\\text{m} / 0.1\\,\\text{s} = 10\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(493,NULL,'This velocity is an average (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(494,NULL,'Max 3 (2 max from MP1–3, 1 max from MP4–5):\n\nThe horizontal distances between the points vary (1)\n\nThe graph is not symmetrical around the vertical **Or** the graph is not parabolic (1)\n\nVelocity-time graph is not a straight line **Or** gradient of velocity-time graph is not constant (1)\n\nHorizontal component of velocity should be constant (1)\n\nAcceleration in vertical direction should equal $g$ (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(495,NULL,'Attempt to find an area under graph (1)\n\nFinds area up to $v = 0$ (1)\n\nHeight in range $2.80$–$2.95\\,\\text{m}$ (1)\n\nExample: area of approximate triangle $= \\frac{1}{2} \\times 0.5 \\times 11.5 = 2.88\\,\\text{m}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
