(505,NULL,'Use of $\\dfrac{1}{R_P} = \\dfrac{1}{R_1} + \\dfrac{1}{R_2}$ for resistors in parallel (1)\n\nUse of $R_T = R_1 + R_2$ for resistors in series (1)\n\n$R_T = 47\\,\\Omega$ (1)\n\nExample: $\\dfrac{1}{R_P} = \\dfrac{1}{30} + \\dfrac{1}{40}$, $R_P = 17\\,\\Omega$; $R_T = 20 + 17 + 10 = 47\\,\\Omega$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(506,NULL,'Use of distance = area under graph (1)\n\nUse of scales on axes (1)\n\nDistance in range $6.0\\,\\text{m}$ – $6.2\\,\\text{m}$ (1)\n\nMP3 dependent on MP1\n\nExample: distance per 2 cm square $= 2.0\\,\\text{m s}^{-1} \\times 0.20\\,\\text{s} = 0.40\\,\\text{m}$; number of squares $= 15$; distance $= 15 \\times 0.40 = 6.0\\,\\text{m}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(507,NULL,'Current (in the circuit) decreases (1)\n\nP.d. across the LDR decreases (1)\n\nVoltmeter reading increases (1)\n\n(MP3 dependent on MP1 or MP2)\n\nAccept potential divider approach: p.d. from supply divides in ratio of resistances, $V_{\\text{LDR}} = \\dfrac{R_{\\text{LDR}}}{R_R + R_{\\text{LDR}}} \\times V_{\\text{Total}}$; as $R_R$ increases but $R_{\\text{LDR}}$ stays constant; so voltmeter reading increases (MP3 dependent on MP1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(508,NULL,'Use $V = IR$ to calculate current (1)\n\nUse $V = IR$ with $V = 6.5\\,\\text{V}$ to calculate LDR resistance (1)\n\nLight intensity $= 185$ (lux) (accept 180 lux – 190 lux) (1)\n\nOR: use of potential divider principle (1); use of $R_T = R_{\\text{LDR}} + 4.5\\,\\text{k}\\Omega$ or $V_{\\text{LDR}} = 6.5\\,\\text{V}$ to calculate LDR resistance (1); light intensity $= 185$ lux (1)\n\nExample: $2.5 = I \\times 4.5 \\times 10^{3}$, $I = 5.6 \\times 10^{-4}\\,\\text{A}$; $6.5 = 0.56 \\times 10^{-3} \\times R$, $R = 11\\,700\\,\\Omega$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(509,NULL,'Correct combination of vectors drawn (1)\n\nBoth vectors drawn to scale (1)\n\nAngle $= 56^{\\circ}$ (range $55^{\\circ}$ – $58^{\\circ}$) (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(510,NULL,'Angle $\\theta$ will need to decrease **Or** (component of boat) velocity along river must increase (1)\n\nThere is further to travel (relative to the water) **Or** (component of boat) velocity across river decreases (1)\n\nTime increases (dependent on either MP1 or MP2) (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(511,NULL,'Max 2 from:\n\nBeam/sling/counterweight also gain kinetic energy (1)\n\nBeam/sling/rock also gain gravitational potential energy (1)\n\nWork done against friction so some energy transferred to surroundings (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(512,NULL,'Use of trig to determine the vertical or horizontal component of the initial velocity (1)\n\nUse of equation(s) of motion to determine the time of flight (1)\n\nUse of $s = ut$ for horizontal motion (1)\n\n$s = 150\\,\\text{m}$ (1)\n\nExample: $u_V = 41.5\\sin 30 = 20.8\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$, $u_H = 41.5\\cos 30 = 35.9\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$; $0 = 20.8 - 9.81t$, $t = 2.12\\,\\text{s}$ (to max height); $s = 35.9 \\times 2 \\times 2.12 = 152\\,\\text{m}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(513,NULL,'Counterweight transfers less gravitational potential energy (1)\n\nSo transfer of kinetic energy to rock is reduced (1)\n\n(When released) rock has smaller vertical (component of) velocity (1)\n\nTime of flight will be reduced (1)\n\n(And) horizontal (component) of velocity will be smaller (1)\n\nAccept references to force and acceleration for MP1 and MP2, e.g. force on rock is decreased, (so) acceleration of rock in sling is decreased.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(514,NULL,'Marks for indicative content (max 4) plus structure and lines of reasoning (max 2).\n\nIC1 The student applies a force to the skateboard\n\nIC2 By Newton\'s 3rd law the skateboard also applies a force to the student\n\nIC3 The two forces are equal in size but opposite in direction\n\nIC4 The student accelerates according to Newton\'s 2nd law\n\nIC5 The skateboard decelerates according to Newton\'s 2nd law\n\nIC6 The skateboard has a higher (magnitude of) acceleration than the student as it has a smaller mass\n\nIC points → IC mark / max linkage / max final: 6 → 4/2/6; 5 → 3/2/5; 4 → 3/1/4; 3 → 2/1/3; 2 → 2/0/2; 1 → 1/0/1; 0 → 0/0/0','MARK_SCHEME'),
(515,NULL,'Use of $E_{\\text{grav}} = mgh$ (1)\n\nUse of $P = IV$ and $P = \\dfrac{E}{t}$ (1)\n\nUse of efficiency $= \\dfrac{\\text{useful power output}}{\\text{total power input}}$ **Or** efficiency $= \\dfrac{\\text{useful energy output}}{\\text{total energy input}}$ (1)\n\nEfficiency $= 0.74$ (1)\n\nExample: $E_P = 0.55 \\times 9.81 \\times 0.20 = 1.08\\,\\text{J}$; useful power $= 1.08/15 = 0.072\\,\\text{W}$; $P = 8.1 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 12 = 0.0972\\,\\text{W}$; input energy $= 0.0972 \\times 15 = 1.46\\,\\text{J}$; efficiency $= 1.08/1.46 = 0.74$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(516,NULL,'There is now a (greater) current in the voltmeter (1)\n\nThe current in the battery/circuit increases **Or** energy/power is dissipated in the voltmeter (1)\n\nPower/energy output from the battery increases (1)\n\nEfficiency of the motor circuit decreases (MP4 dependent on MP3) (1)\n\nAccept reference to the resistance in the circuit decreasing for MP1. Accept power input to circuit for MP3.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(517,NULL,'Use of $p = mv$ (1)\n\nUse of principle of conservation of momentum (1)\n\n$v = 5.2\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$ (1)\n\nExample: $0.55 \\times 5.4 = (0.55 \\times 2.1) + (0.35 \\times v)$; $2.97 = 1.16 + 0.35v$; $v = 5.2\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(518,NULL,'Either:\n\nAs $x$ increases, the (clockwise) moment of the load about P increases (1)\n\n(For equilibrium) the clockwise moment and the anticlockwise moment about P must be equal (1)\n\nSo $F_Q$ must increase (to increase the anticlockwise moment) (MP3 dependent on MP2) (1)\n\n(For equilibrium) the resultant vertical force must be zero **Or** as $x$ increases, the (anticlockwise) moment of the load about Q decreases (1)\n\nAs $F_Q$ increases $F_P$ must decrease (1)\n\nOR:\n\nAs $x$ increases, the (anticlockwise) moment of the load about Q decreases (1)\n\n(For equilibrium) the clockwise moment and the anticlockwise moment about Q must be equal (1)\n\nSo $F_P$ must decrease (to decrease the clockwise moment) (MP3 dependent on MP2) (1)\n\n(For equilibrium) the resultant vertical force must be zero **Or** as $x$ increases, the (clockwise) moment of the load about P increases (1)\n\nAs $F_P$ decreases $F_Q$ must increase (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(519,NULL,'Ensure the metre rule is vertical using a set square placed in contact with the floor (1)\n\nUse set square held against metre rule to take the readings on the metre rule (1)\n\nA labelled diagram can score full marks. Accept references to reading at eye level for MP2.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(520,NULL,'Use of half range **Or** use of maximum deviation from the mean (1)\n\n$0.4\\,(\\%)$ (1)\n\nExample: $\\tfrac{1}{2}$ range $= (6.29 - 6.24)/2 = 0.025\\,\\text{mm}$; $\\%U = (0.025/6.26) \\times 100 = 0.40\\,\\%$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(521,NULL,'Correct values for $l^{3}$ to 3 s.f. (1)\n\nAxes with labels & units (1)\n\nScales (1)\n\nPlots (1)\n\nLine of best fit (1)\n\n$l^{3}$ / m$^{3}$: 0.027(0), 0.064(0), 0.125, 0.216, 0.343, 0.512\n\nMP1: accept 2 or 3 s.f. in table for first 2 values. MP3: scales only in 1, 2, 5 and must cover at least half of paper. MP4: a 1 mm square tolerance, check all points.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(522,NULL,'Determine gradient (1)\n\nUsing a large triangle (1)\n\nUse of gradient $= \\dfrac{4W}{Ebt^{3}}$ (1)\n\n$E = 9.2 \\times 10^{9}\\,(\\text{Pa})$ (in range $8.9$ – $9.3 \\times 10^{9}\\,\\text{Pa}$) (1)\n\nIdentifies wood consistent with their value for $E$ (1)\n\nExample: gradient $= \\dfrac{(160 \\times 10^{-3}) - 0}{0.50 - 0.01} = 0.327$; $0.327 = \\dfrac{4 \\times 5.6}{E \\times 0.0302 \\times (6.26 \\times 10^{-3})^{3}}$; $E = 9.2 \\times 10^{9}\\,\\text{Pa}$ (Douglas fir)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(523,NULL,'Use of $\\rho = \\dfrac{m}{V}$ (1)\n\n$V = 8.1 \\times 10^{-6}\\,(\\text{m}^{3})$ (1)\n\nExample: $V = \\dfrac{0.043\\,\\text{kg}}{5300\\,\\text{kg m}^{-3}} = 8.1 \\times 10^{-6}\\,\\text{m}^{3}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(524,NULL,'Use of $A = \\pi r^{2}$ and $V = Al$ (1)\n\nUse of $R = \\dfrac{\\rho l}{A}$ (1)\n\n$R = 2.5\\,\\Omega$ (ecf from (a)(i)) (1)\n\nShow that value gives $2.50\\,\\Omega$\n\nExample: $A = \\pi(6 \\times 10^{-3})^{2} = 1.13 \\times 10^{-4}\\,\\text{m}^{2}$; $8.1 \\times 10^{-6} = 1.13 \\times 10^{-4} \\times l$, $l = 0.0716\\,\\text{m}$; $R = \\dfrac{4.0 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 0.0716}{1.13 \\times 10^{-4}} = 2.54\\,\\Omega$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(525,NULL,'Makes a cylinder of longer length **Or** makes a cylinder of smaller radius/area (1)\n\nAs $R = \\dfrac{\\rho l}{A}$, this change will increase the resistance (1)\n\n[MP2 dependent on MP1]','MARK_SCHEME'),
