(536,NULL,'Straight horizontal line at $-2\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$ (1)\n\nEnding at $38.5\\,\\text{s}$ (1)\n\nExample: time of descent $= 67.5/2 = 33.8\\,\\text{s}$; end of motion $= 33.8 + 4.5 = 38.3\\,\\text{s}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(537,NULL,'Scale to cover at least half of the grid either vertically or horizontally (1)\n\nDownward and horizontal velocity arrows **and** corresponding resultant velocity arrow drawn (1)\n\nMagnitude of velocity $= 2.5\\,(\\text{m s}^{-1})$ (1)\n\nAngle to the horizontal $= 53^\\circ$ [allow $\\pm 1^\\circ$] (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(538,NULL,'Power source in series with coil of wire and ammeter **and** voltmeter correctly placed **or** ohmmeter in series with wire (1)\n\nAllow power supply/cell/battery. Wire must be clear. Ignore extra components as long as circuit would still work.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(539,NULL,'Measure temperature of water with a thermometer (1)\n\nStir the water **or** keep thermometer near to coil **or** allow time to ensure coil is at thermal equilibrium **or** use small current (so electrical heating of wire is minimised) (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(540,NULL,'Kinetic energy of (lattice) ions increases **or** (lattice) ions vibrate with greater amplitude (1)\n\nThe frequency of collisions between electrons and ions increases (1)\n\n(Drift) velocity of electrons decreases, so current decreases (for the same p.d.) (1)\n\nSo resistance of nichrome wire increases [dependent upon MP1, MP2 or MP3] (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(541,NULL,'Calculates area (1)\n\nUse of $R = \\dfrac{\\rho l}{A}$ (1)\n\n$l = 10\\,\\text{m}$ (1)\n\nExample: $A = \\pi (0.28 \\times 10^{-3}\\,\\text{m})^2 / 4 = 6.2 \\times 10^{-8}\\,\\text{m}^2$; $80\\,\\Omega = (4.9 \\times 10^{-7}\\,\\Omega\\,\\text{m} \\times l) / 6.2 \\times 10^{-8}\\,\\text{m}^2$; $l = 10.1\\,\\text{m}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(542,NULL,'**Either:**\n\nPercentage uncertainty calculated for either $I$ or $V$ (1)\n\nPercentage uncertainties for $I$ and $V$ added (1)\n\nLower limit of $R = 74\\,\\Omega$ **or** uncertainty in $R = 8\\,\\Omega$ (1)\n\nConclusion consistent with calculated value of lower limit (1)\n\n**Or:**\n\n(Upper/lower) limit of $V$ calculated **and** (upper/lower) limit of $I$ calculated (1)\n\nMinimum value of $R$ calculated **or** maximum value of $R$ calculated (1)\n\nLower limit of $R = 75\\,\\Omega$ (1)\n\nConclusion consistent with calculated value of lower limit (1)\n\nExample: $\\%U_I = 8.3\\%$, $\\%U_V = 1.0\\%$, $R = 9.8/0.12 = 82\\,\\Omega$, $\\%U_R = 9.3\\%$, lower limit $= 82 \\times (1 - 0.093) = 74\\,\\Omega$; $80\\,\\Omega$ is within limit, so agrees with experimental results.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(543,NULL,'Working leading to $W = 245\\,(\\text{N})$ (1)\n\nExample: $W = 25.0\\,\\text{kg} \\times 9.81\\,\\text{m s}^{-2} = 245\\,\\text{N}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(544,NULL,'Use of moment of force $= Fx$ (1)\n\nUse of the principle of moments (1)\n\nSupport force $= 170\\,\\text{N}$ (ecf from (a)(i)) (1)\n\nExample: $245\\,\\text{N} \\times 2.5\\,\\text{m} = F \\times 3.6\\,\\text{m}$, $F = 170\\,\\text{N}$ (show that value gives $174\\,\\text{N}$)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(545,NULL,'When plank tips, support force at rock $= 0$ **or** when plank tips, clockwise moment is greater than anticlockwise moment (1)\n\nUse of principle of moments (1)\n\nIf person is at the end, clockwise moment $= 770\\,\\text{N m}$, anticlockwise moment due to weight is $270\\,\\text{N m} < 770\\,\\text{N m}$ so student is correct **or** starts to tip when person is $0.49\\,\\text{m}$ to the right of the wall, $0.49\\,\\text{m} < 1.4\\,\\text{m}$ so student is correct (1)\n\nExample: $245\\,\\text{N} \\times (2.5 - 1.4)\\,\\text{m} = 550\\,\\text{N} \\times x$, $x = 0.49\\,\\text{m} < 1.4\\,\\text{m}$ so tips','MARK_SCHEME'),
(546,NULL,'Marks for indicative content (max 4) plus structure and lines of reasoning (max 2).\n\n**Indicative content:**\n\nIC1 The resistance of the circuit decreases (as more bulbs are added) **or** there are more paths for the current\n\nIC2 (So) current in battery increases\n\nIC3 p.d. across internal resistance increases\n\nIC4 Terminal p.d. of battery decreases and bulbs get dimmer\n\nIC5 (More) energy/power transferred in battery\n\nIC6 So thermal energy increases in battery\n\nIC points to marks: 6 → 4 (+2 linkage), 5 → 3 (+2), 4 → 3 (+1), 3 → 2 (+1), 2 → 2 (+0), 1 → 1 (+0).','MARK_SCHEME'),
(547,NULL,'Use of $p = mv$ (1)\n\n$p = 5.4\\,(\\text{kg m s}^{-1})$ (1)\n\nExample: $p = 0.012\\,\\text{kg} \\times 450\\,\\text{m s}^{-1} = 5.4\\,\\text{kg m s}^{-1}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(548,NULL,'Use of principle of conservation of linear momentum (1)\n\nUse of $\\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2 = mgh$ (1)\n\n$h = 0.24\\,\\text{m}$ (ecf from (a)(i)) (1)\n\nExample: $5.4 = (2.5 + 0.012) \\times v$, $v = 2.1\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$; $\\tfrac{1}{2}(2.1)^2 = 9.81 \\times h$, $h = 0.24\\,\\text{m}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(549,NULL,'The final momentum/velocity of the bullet is negative **or** in the opposite direction (1)\n\nThe change in momentum of the bullet is greater (1)\n\nThe (change in) momentum of the steel block is greater (because momentum is conserved) (1)\n\nSo steel block has greater initial velocity **or** greater initial $E_\\text{K}$ (1)\n\nSo gains greater $E_\\text{P}$ (and moves through a greater maximum vertical height) [dependent upon MP4] (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(550,NULL,'Use of a metre rule (1)\n\nUse of a set square to ensure the rule is vertical **or** use of plumbline to ensure that the rule is vertical (1)\n\nAllow use of a spirit level for MP2.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(551,NULL,'Comparison of $h = \\dfrac{v^2}{2g}$ with $y = mx + c$ (1)\n\nGradient $= \\dfrac{1}{2g}$ which is constant (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(552,NULL,'Values of $v^2$ given to 3 s.f. (1)\n\nAxes with labels & units (1)\n\nScales (1)\n\nPlots (1)\n\nLine of best fit (1)\n\n$v^2$ / $\\text{m}^2\\,\\text{s}^{-2}$: 1.90, 3.92, 6.00, 8.18, 10.37, 11.97\n\nMP1 allow 4 s.f. for final two values in table. MP3: scales only in 1, 2 or 5 and must cover at least half of paper.','MARK_SCHEME'),
(553,NULL,'Use of large triangle (1)\n\nUse of gradient $= \\dfrac{1}{2g}$ (1)\n\n$g$ in range $10.0$–$10.9\\,(\\text{m s}^{-2})$ and relevant comment (1)\n\nExample: gradient $= (0.580 - 0.005) / 12.0 = 0.0479$; $g = 1 / (2 \\times 0.0479) = 10.4\\,\\text{m s}^{-2}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
