(607,NULL,'Use of $v = u + at$ (1)\n\nUse of $v\\cos\\theta$ (1)\n\nUse of $s = ut + \\tfrac{1}{2}at^2$ (1)\n\nNumber of body lengths = range / body length (1)\n\n12.7 body lengths, so not twenty lengths (1)\n\nExample: $t = \\dfrac{(0 - 2.2)\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}}{-9.8\\,\\text{m s}^{-2}} = 0.224\\,\\text{s}$; $v\\cos\\theta = 2.6\\,\\text{m s}^{-1} \\times \\cos 57^\\circ = 1.42\\,\\text{m s}^{-1}$; $s = 1.42\\,\\text{m s}^{-1} \\times 2 \\times 0.224\\,\\text{s} = 0.636\\,\\text{m}$; number of lengths $= \\dfrac{0.636\\,\\text{m}}{0.05\\,\\text{m}} = 12.7$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(608,NULL,'Energy released does not change, so same final velocity (1)\n\nSame increase in velocity over longer time means a smaller acceleration (1)\n\nSmaller force exerted by legs during jump (1)\n\nSo smaller force on ground (1)\n\nMP3 and MP4 dependent upon MP2\n\nAlternative: energy released does not change so work done does not change (1); work done = force × displacement (1); as legs are longer, force exerted by legs during jump is smaller (1); so smaller force on ground (1)','MARK_SCHEME'),
(609,NULL,'Vertical velocity component increases by factor of 1.2, so time in air increases by a factor of 1.2 (1)\n\nHorizontal component of velocity increases by factor of 1.2 (1)\n\nHorizontal distance travelled increases by a factor of $1.2 \\times 1.2 = 1.44$ (1)\n\nSo jump length would increase by $44\\%$, not by $100\\%$ (1)\n\nOR: (20% increase in $v$ means that) new jump velocity $= 1.2v$ (1); use of expressions for time to greatest height and horizontal distance (1); jump length is proportional to $v^2$ (1); new jump length $= 1.44 \\times$ original jump length, so not double the length (1)\n\nExample: $u \\to 1.2u$, $t \\to 1.2t$, $s = ut$, so $s \\to 1.2u \\times 1.2t = 1.44s$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(610,NULL,'Use of $\\Delta F = k\\Delta x$ (1)\n\n$45\\,(\\text{N m}^{-1})$ (1) [accept $0.45\\,\\text{N cm}^{-1}$]\n\nExample: $k = \\dfrac{2.5\\,\\text{N}}{5.5 \\times 10^{-2}\\,\\text{m}} = 45.45\\,\\text{N m}^{-1}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(611,NULL,'Use of $w = mg$ (1)\n\nUse of vertical component of spring forces (1)\n\nUse of $\\Delta F = k\\Delta x$ (1)\n\n$\\Delta x = 0.050\\,\\text{m}$ (1) [accept $5.0\\,\\text{cm}$] (ECF from (a)(i))\n\nExample: $w = 0.400\\,\\text{kg} \\times 9.8\\,\\text{N kg}^{-1} = 3.92\\,\\text{N}$; $2T\\cos 30^\\circ = 3.92\\,\\text{N}$, so $F = \\dfrac{3.92\\,\\text{N}}{2\\cos 30^\\circ} = 2.26\\,\\text{N}$; $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2.26\\,\\text{N}}{45.45\\,\\text{N m}^{-1}} = 0.0498\\,\\text{m}$','MARK_SCHEME'),
(612,NULL,'Marks are awarded for indicative content (IC) and for how the answer is structured and shows lines of reasoning.\n\nIndicative content:\n\n- There is an upthrust on the rock (as the rock displaces water)\n- Hence the net downward force on the rock is reduced\n- F decreases to a minimum once the rock is fully submerged\n- The rock pushes downwards on the water\n- So there is an extra force on the balance\n- Hence R increases to a maximum once the rock is fully submerged\n\nIC points → IC mark + max linkage mark = max final mark: 6 → 4 + 2 = 6; 5 → 3 + 2 = 5; 4 → 3 + 1 = 4; 3 → 2 + 1 = 3; 2 → 2 + 0 = 2; 1 → 1 + 0 = 1; 0 → 0','MARK_SCHEME'),
