(628,21,4,'10(b)',11,'THEORY',4,'The maximum speed limit on motorways has not been raised since the 1960s. However, developments in engineering mean that modern cars have much higher top speeds than cars in the past and can stop safely in shorter distances.\n\nDiscuss the risks and benefits of raising the maximum speed limits on motorways.',NULL,26,39,'Risks and benefits of scientific decisions','MEDIUM',8,'[8]','0.90','PUBLISHED',NULL,'2026-10-05 18:26:14',NULL,'[358,486,382,550,581,575,381,385,339,485]','2026-10-05 18:26:35',NULL),
(629,21,4,'11(a)',12,'STRUCTURED',5,'Extinct animals can be studied by using their fossils. 70-million-year-old fossils from the *Tyrannosaurus rex* and *Triceratops* dinosaurs show that a *Triceratops* was sometimes eaten by a *Tyrannosaurus rex*.\n\nThe diagram shows a *Tyrannosaurus rex* skull.\n\nOn the diagram, the position of the main biting muscle is indicated by the line labelled \'muscle force\'. The muscle is connected to the jaw at point J. This produces a moment about point P where the jaw is hinged. Teeth marks found in fossilised *Triceratops* bones show that the force exerted by a tooth at the front of the jaw X could reach $6500\\,\\text{N}$.\n\nThe skull is drawn to a scale of 1 to 10. The force arrows are **not** drawn to scale.\n\n(a) Take measurements from the diagram to determine the size of the muscle force when the force exerted by the tooth at X is $6500\\,\\text{N}$.',NULL,27,47,'Moments','HARD',10,'[10,11]','0.85','PUBLISHED',NULL,'2026-10-05 18:26:14',NULL,'[473,474,348,592,526,419,573,544,450,412]','2026-10-05 18:26:35',NULL),
(630,21,4,'11(b)',13,'THEORY',3,'The length of a tooth from another dinosaur is approximately $10\\,\\text{cm}$.\n\nScientist A measures this length with a metre rule, and scientist B measures this length with callipers.\n\nScientist B claims that his measurement will produce a more accurate value for the length of the tooth.\n\nComment on the claim made by scientist B.',NULL,26,37,'Accuracy and uncertainty','MEDIUM',11,'[11]','0.90','PUBLISHED',NULL,'2026-10-05 18:26:14',NULL,'[379,550,439,582,520,583,405,519,572,499]','2026-10-05 18:26:35',NULL),
(631,21,4,'12(a)',14,'THEORY',3,'A potential difference is applied across the metal filament of a light bulb and charge flows.\n\n(a) By referring to the mean drift velocity of the electrons, explain what happens to the current in the metal filament if the potential difference is unchanged and the temperature of the metal increases.',NULL,28,57,'Drift velocity and resistance','MEDIUM',13,'[13]','0.90','PUBLISHED',NULL,'2026-10-05 18:26:14',NULL,'[436,500,389,562,391,457,390,403,380,546]','2026-10-05 18:26:35',NULL),
(632,21,4,'12(b)(i)',15,'STRUCTURED',2,'The graph shows the variation of current $I$ with potential difference $V$ for two electrical components X and Y.\n\nX is a filament bulb and Y is a fixed resistor.\n\nA potential divider circuit consisting of components X and Y is connected to a $9.0\\,\\text{V}$ supply in series with a fixed resistor R as shown. The supply has a negligible internal resistance.\n\nThe reading on the voltmeter is $3.0\\,\\text{V}$.\n\n(i) Determine the current in the fixed resistor R.',NULL,28,55,'I-V characteristics and parallel circuits','MEDIUM',14,'[14,15]','0.90','PUBLISHED',NULL,'2026-10-05 18:26:14',NULL,'[404,410,577,389,390,375,441,346,355,508]','2026-10-05 18:26:35',NULL),
